The logs show the root cause:

Plain Text
Server startup in [2061] milliseconds
...
Deploying web application archive [/usr/local/tomcat/webapps/ROOT.war]
...
Deployment of web application archive [...] has finished
...
Starting ProtocolHandler ["http-nio-8080"]

Then about 24 seconds later:

Plain Text
Pausing ProtocolHandler ["http-nio-8080"]
Stopping service [Catalina]
Destroying ProtocolHandler ["http-nio-8080"]
What this means

✅ Tomcat starts successfully
 ✅ WAR file deploys successfully
 ✅ Application starts successfully
 ✅ Image is OK

❌ Something is stopping the container after startup.

This is usually caused by one of these:

Check pod events

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe pod myapp1-74ff77bfcd-tqpxl

Look for:

Plain Text
Liveness probe failed
Readiness probe failed
OOMKilled
Killing container

at the bottom under Events.

Check deployment configuration

Run:

Shell
kubectl get deployment myapp1 -o yaml

Look for something like:

YAML
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 80

If your container listens on:

Plain Text
8080

but probe checks:

Plain Text
80

then Kubernetes will continuously kill the pod.

A correct probe would be:

YAML
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 8080
 
readinessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 8080
Check if pod is OOMKilled

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe pod myapp1-74ff77bfcd-tqpxl | grep -A5 State
 

If you see:

Plain Text
Reason: OOMKilled

increase memory:

YAML
resources:
limits:
memory: 1024Mi
requests:
memory: 512Mi
Most important command now

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe pod myapp1-74ff77bfcd-tqpxl

and share the Events section at the bottom.

The logs prove the Java application starts successfully, so the next clue is in the pod events showing why Kubernetes is killing it.

